---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import CaseStudyNav from '../../components/CaseStudyNav.astro';
import journeyMap1 from '../../assets/images/journey-map-1.png';
import journeyMap2 from '../../assets/images/journey-map-2.png';
import raaJourney from '../../assets/images/raa-journey.png';
import raaPersona from '../../assets/images/raa-persona.png';
---
<Layout title="Journey Mapping | Andrew Smith" description="Journey mapping as a practice, connecting analytics, research, and team knowledge into a shared source of truth for member experience.">
  <a class="skip-link" href="#main-content">Skip to content</a>
  <div class="case-page">
    <SiteHeader />
    <main id="main-content">
      <section class="case-hero">
        <p class="eyebrow"><a class="breadcrumb-link" href="/portfolio/work">Work</a><span class="breadcrumb-separator">/</span><span>Journey Mapping</span></p>
        <h1 class="display-title">Making the experience visible so decisions are based on evidence, not assumption.</h1>
        <p class="hero-text">Most organisations have more data about their customers than they can make sense of. Analytics sitting in one platform, research findings in another, NPS and customer feedback somewhere else entirely, and no way to connect any of it to the specific moment in the product where the experience is breaking down.</p>
        <p class="hero-text">Journey mapping solves that. A well-maintained journey map is a single shared view of what is known about the customer experience: stages, steps, emotional states, pain points, opportunities, and the metrics that tell you whether things are improving or getting worse. The difference between a team that argues about priorities based on who speaks most confidently, and a team that argues about priorities based on what the data says.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="meta-strip">
        <div class="meta-item"><p class="meta-label">Scope</p><p class="meta-value">Insurance, membership, travel, identity</p></div>
        <div class="meta-item"><p class="meta-label">Role</p><p class="meta-value">Senior UX Design Lead</p></div>
        <div class="meta-item"><p class="meta-label">Tools</p><p class="meta-value">Journey management platform, Miro, FigJam</p></div>
      </section>
      <section class="lead-image-section">
        <Image class="lead-image" src={journeyMap1} alt="Journey explorer showing phases, win rates, drop-offs, emotion curve, and prioritised solutions with member and business value ratings" decoding="async" widths={[720,1080,1440]} sizes="(max-width:78rem) 100vw, 78rem"/>
      </section>

      <section class="summary-section">
        <p class="section-kicker">Why Journey Management Matters</p>
        <h2>A journey map is useful when it stays connected to real data and real decisions.</h2>
        <div class="summary-main">
          <p>The cost of not having journey management is not immediately visible. Products get designed and shipped in fragments. A payment flow is improved without knowing how it connects to the login experience that preceded it, or the confirmation email that follows it. Metrics improve in one area and degrade in another, and nobody has the context to understand why.</p>
          <p>Journey management builds that context. At the enterprise level, it provides a shared view across all products, so every team can see where their product fits in the broader member experience and how changes in one area affect others. At the nested level, it maps the detail: logging in, searching for a product, making a payment, lodging a claim. Each of those is its own journey with its own pain points and its own data.</p>
          <p>AI tools have made it faster to build and maintain these maps. Analytics can be mined across journeys to surface patterns. Research insights can be synthesised and tagged to specific steps. Gaps in knowledge: stages where drop-offs are happening but there is no research explaining why , become visible immediately rather than being discovered months later when a redesign fails to improve the metric.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-section">
        <div class="section-rail"><p class="section-kicker">Metrics-Connected Maps</p></div>
        <div class="story-block">
          <p>The journey explorer above connects real analytics to each stage of the experience: win rates, drop-off rates, failure rates, member emotion scores. Solutions are prioritised by member value and business value, and the map shows which ones are on the roadmap, which are in delivery, and which are on hold.</p>
          <p>This is what a journey map looks like when it is actually used, not a slide in a presentation, but a live tool that shapes what gets worked on next. When a metric shifts, the team can see exactly which stage is affected and what the current prioritised solutions are for that stage.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="supporting-image-section">
        <Image class="supporting-image" src={journeyMap2} alt="Journey explorer with solution detail panel open showing priority rating, effort, member value, and business value" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[720,1080,1440]} sizes="(max-width:78rem) 100vw, 78rem"/>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-section">
        <div class="section-rail"><p class="section-kicker">Research-Grounded Maps</p></div>
        <div class="story-block">
          <p>Before digital journey management tools, maps were built from research synthesis, combining interviews, usability sessions, analytics, and team knowledge into a shared artefact, that captured everything known about a specific journey. The map below shows a full member experience from initial awareness through to post-booking , stages, emotional states, touchpoints, pain points, and opportunities at each step.</p>
          <p>Maps like this serve two purposes. They align the team around a shared understanding of the experience as it actually is, not as it was designed to be. And they surface the gaps: the stages where the team is making decisions without adequate research, and where investment in discovery would have the greatest impact.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="supporting-image-section">
        <Image class="supporting-image" src={raaJourney} alt="Journey map showing member stages from research through booking with moods, touchpoints, pain points, and opportunities" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[720,1080,1440]} sizes="(max-width:78rem) 100vw, 78rem"/>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-section">
        <div class="section-rail"><p class="section-kicker">Personas</p></div>
        <div class="story-block">
          <p>A journey map without a persona is a description of an experience without a person having it. Personas ground every design decision in a real human with real motivations, real constraints, and real expectations. so when the team is deciding where to invest in the experience, the question is not "what do users want" but "what does this specific person need at this specific moment, and what does it cost them when we get it wrong.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="supporting-image-section">
        <Image class="supporting-image" src={raaPersona} alt="User persona with background, travel goals, research behaviour, likes, dislikes, and device usage" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[720,1080,1440]} sizes="(max-width:78rem) 100vw, 78rem"/>
      </section>
      <CaseStudyNav />
    </main>
  </div>
</Layout>
<style>
.case-page{position:relative;width:min(calc(100% - 3rem),78rem);margin:0 auto;padding:1.25rem 0 5rem}
.eyebrow{font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:.82rem;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted-foreground);margin:0 0 1.25rem;display:flex;gap:.5rem;align-items:center}
.breadcrumb-link{font-weight:600}.breadcrumb-separator{color:var(--muted-foreground)}
h1,h2{margin:0;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:-.05em;color:var(--foreground)}
.display-title{font-size:clamp(2rem,4.5vw,3.5rem);line-height:1.06;margin:1rem 0 1.5rem;max-width:28ch}
.hero-subtext{font-size:1.05rem;line-height:1.65;color:var(--muted-foreground);font-style:italic;margin:.25rem 0 0;max-width:42rem}
.hero-text,.story-block p,.summary-main p{font-size:1.08rem;line-height:1.7;margin:0 0 1rem}
.case-hero{padding:5rem 0 2rem}
.meta-strip{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr));gap:2rem;padding:1.5rem 0 .5rem;border-top:1px solid var(--border)}
.meta-label{font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:.78rem;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted-foreground);margin:0}
.meta-value{margin:.35rem 0 0;font-size:1.1rem;font-weight:700}
.lead-image-section{padding:2rem 0 4rem}
.lead-image,.supporting-image{width:100%;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:1.5rem;box-shadow:var(--shadow-large)}
.supporting-image-section{padding:2rem 0 4rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)}
.summary-section{padding:0 0 3rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)}
.summary-section h2{margin-top:.75rem;font-size:clamp(1.5rem,2.5vw,2.2rem);max-width:32ch}
.summary-main{margin-top:1.5rem;max-width:44rem}
.section-kicker{font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:.78rem;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted-foreground);margin:0 0 .5rem}
.editorial-section{display:grid;grid-template-columns:14rem minmax(0,1fr);gap:2rem;padding:3rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)}
.story-block{max-width:44rem}
@media(max-width:860px){.meta-strip{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:1rem}.editorial-section{grid-template-columns:1fr}}
</style>
